From: OpenWrt scratch debug
Subject: DEBUG: net: airoha: disable FE VIP force-to-CPU for BOOTP patterns

DEBUG-only diagnostic patch, part of an investigation into a silent
DHCP-reply (and possibly PPPoE PADO) receive loss on the Nokia
XG-040G-MF (Airoha AN7583).

airoha_fe_vip_setup() programs two FE "VIP" pattern-match entries
(REG_FE_VIP_PATN/EN 8 and 9) that force BOOTP traffic (UDP src-port
67 and 68) onto a special force-to-CPU classification path
(PATN_FCPU_EN_MASK), independent of the normal per-port hash-based
RX queue selection that all other traffic uses. That path routes
into RX queue 4 (REG_CDM_FWD_CFG CDM_VIP_QSEL_MASK=0x4). The same
mechanism (PATN_FCPU_EN_MASK, same RX queue 4) also force-routes
PPPoE Discovery frames (ethertype 0x8863, REG_FE_VIP_PATN(3)).

A field investigation with dual-sided pcap captured simultaneously on
the Nokia XG-040G-MF and on the upstream DHCP server proved that
DHCP-server replies are transmitted onto the wire (confirmed in the
server's own capture, broadcast dst-MAC, replies within single-digit
milliseconds) but never arrive at the MF's own network stack - not
even visible to tcpdump/AF_PACKET on the WAN port, while unrelated
broadcast traffic from the same source MAC on the same wire in the
same window is received normally. A companion RX-drop trace patch
(925-DEBUG-an7583-trace-rx-classify-drop.patch) never fires for this
failure, meaning the frame never becomes a QDMA RX descriptor at all -
the loss happens upstream of the driver's software RX loop, in FE
hardware. Once triggered, the loss does not self-recover (observed
continuously broken for tens of minutes across multiple
reproductions) until the interface or the board is restarted.

BOOTP is the only VIP pattern matched purely by UDP source port
(PATN_SP_EN_MASK, no ethertype/DP match) among this table, and is
the only one whose "force to CPU" queue (4) is also nominally
LRO-range in the driver's queue layout - both are candidate causes,
not confirmed root causes.

This patch removes PATN_FCPU_EN_MASK from both BOOTP entries (8 and
9), leaving PATN_EN_MASK/PATN_SP_EN_MASK/PATN_TYPE_MASK untouched.
BOOTP traffic should then be classified through the same path as
all other successfully-received traffic instead of being
force-routed to CPU queue 4. This is a diagnostic experiment to
isolate whether the FE VIP force-to-CPU mechanism itself (rather
than queue 4/LRO specifically) is where the loss occurs.

Not for upstream submission - diagnostic-only, changes hardware
classification behavior for BOOTP/DHCP traffic.

---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
@@ -221,13 +221,15 @@
 	/* BOOTP (0x43) */
 	airoha_fe_wr(eth, REG_FE_VIP_PATN(8), 0x43);
 	airoha_fe_wr(eth, REG_FE_VIP_EN(8),
-		     PATN_FCPU_EN_MASK | PATN_SP_EN_MASK |
+		     /* DEBUG: force-to-CPU intentionally disabled here */
+		     PATN_SP_EN_MASK |
 		     FIELD_PREP(PATN_TYPE_MASK, 4) | PATN_EN_MASK);
 
 	/* BOOTP (0x44) */
 	airoha_fe_wr(eth, REG_FE_VIP_PATN(9), 0x44);
 	airoha_fe_wr(eth, REG_FE_VIP_EN(9),
-		     PATN_FCPU_EN_MASK | PATN_SP_EN_MASK |
+		     /* DEBUG: force-to-CPU intentionally disabled here */
+		     PATN_SP_EN_MASK |
 		     FIELD_PREP(PATN_TYPE_MASK, 4) | PATN_EN_MASK);
 
 	/* ISAKMP */
